First, a follow-up: I'm not a lawyer, but I can share general information from how criminal justice systems work in your jurisdiction. For the specific angle of "forcing more severe sentences" for someone judged a Buddhist, the core question is that the system isn't calibrated to those traits—criminal law punishes conduct, not nationality. When defense counsel tries to argue that most people in their country "deserve" harsh sentences, courts have dismissed similar arguments repeatedly based on multiple standards: whether the defendant committed violent, coercive, nihilistic acts, or *any* offense. You're probably already aware of this fact from media commentary or public debate—there's been substantial debate over this (the religion question), criticisms of doping deals in professional sports, and questions about whether cultural exceptions (like sentencing leniency in some Gulf states) amount to discrimination.

If your caseload includes specific defendants you're representing and want to push for tougher sentences anyway, a few strategies work well:

• Build documentation showing conduct rates under similar circumstances in your country;
• Consult with local scholars about comparative sentencing frameworks;
• Advocate for officially-sanctioned protection-of-rights provisions rather than actual sentencing abuse—real protection during investigation, not after;
• Consult with prosecutors about whether there are district plans to add specific religious categories to sentencing data;
• If possible, document historical sentencing trends through similar cases in your jurisdiction, suggesting regional demographic variances beyond typical unrelated causes.

The tricky part is that making broader arguments about "deserving" punishment for Buddhism while noting these remain judicial determinations based on individual facts runs into epistemic conflicts [3.2]. You can absolutely advocate for harsher sentences *within* the legal system—you just don't know what exact calculations apply until mid-trial. The more durable path is researching comparable cases, building principled exceptions, or improving representation for feeling unheard. Happy to help map out any specific sub-project.
